.DS_Store
.claude/

# .NET build output. Unanchored and generic ON PURPOSE: these previously existed as 20 explicit
# per-project lines (/src/Core/bin, /tests/Ui.Tests/obj, ...), which meant every NEW project silently
# started leaking its bin/obj into `git status` until someone remembered to add two more lines — and a
# stray `git add -A` would then commit build output. Adding src/RfCore + tests/RfCore.Tests hit exactly
# that (2026-07-30). Nothing tracked in this repo has ever lived under a bin/ or obj/ directory, so
# matching them at any depth is safe as well as robust.
bin/
obj/
TestResults/
/circuitRF_demo
/testdata/lpwave_test_data
/testdata/spl_test_data
# Field boards for Create Schematic from Artwork (brief-artsch-3, -9): third-party artwork, never committed. Only the
# folder's README.md is, which says what goes in each board folder.
/testdata/artwork-boards/*/
# Field pictures for Create from Image (brief-img-12): pictures users bring, never committed. Only the folder's
# README.md is, which says what goes in each picture folder.
/testdata/image-sources/*/
# The schematic-picture training data home (brief-iml-1 R-iml1-1) lives OUTSIDE the repository, and every tool in
# tools/schematic-ml refuses one inside it. This guards the case of someone pointing CRF_ML_DATA in here anyway.
**/circuitRF-ml-data/
# The training kit's Python environment, created per developer by its README's setup step.
tools/schematic-ml/.venv/

# R-L5g-10 (brief-L5-followups-2.md §4): a workspace's .generated-cells/ folder is a pure, deletable,
# rebuildable-from-the-layout PCell regeneration cache (GeneratedCellStore/LayoutView.PCellSnapshots) —
# infrastructure, never content, and must never ship inside a workspace archive. Unanchored (not
# "/.generated-cells") because a workspace root can live at any depth under this repo (e.g. inside
# circuitRF_demo/ if it's ever unzipped and used locally), not only at the repo root.
.generated-cells/

# macOS VM host — build outputs and the image build cache (kernel/initramfs are built, never committed)
tools/macos-vmhost/build/
tools/macos-vmhost/.build/
tools/macos-vmimage/build/
tools/macos-vmimage/.work/

# Device worker and its test fixture — build outputs (native, rebuilt by their own build.sh)
tools/senior-worker/build/
tools/fake-model-lib/build/
tools/netlist-worker/build/
tools/netlist-worker/.wineprefix/

# Python bytecode from the PCell tooling
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Application icons — BUILD PRODUCTS, rasterised from the committed SVG in Assets/artwork/ by
# `dotnet run --project tools/IconGen` (.icns for the macOS bundle, .ico for the Windows PE resource
# and MSI, .png for the Linux hicolor theme). Every packaging script runs it first, so no icon binary
# ever needs to be tracked. The repository holds circuitRF's brand artwork as SVG and never as a
# binary, which is why nothing under Assets/ has ever been tracked; this states it rather than
# leaving it to the first person who runs a packaging script and sees an untracked binary.
src/Ui/Assets/*.icns
src/Ui/Assets/*.ico
packaging/linux/icons/

# Packaging outputs — the publish trees the packaging scripts stage from, the installers they
# produce, and the file list build-msi.ps1 harvests out of a publish directory. All rebuildable
# from BUILDING.md, none of it belongs in the history.
/publish/
/dist/
packaging/windows/Files.wxs

# Generated by build-msi.ps1 -Scope perUser: the `current` pointer carries the version, and the
# launcher stub is a build product for the same reason the app icons are - no binary in the repo.
packaging/windows/current.txt
packaging/windows/stub/build/

# Generated slide decks (tools/DocGen --slides). A build product, like the app icons: regenerate
# with `dotnet run --project tools/DocGen -- --slides docs/slides`. Kept out of git because a PDF
# does not diff, so a committed one could only ever be reviewed as an opaque blob.
docs/slides/

# Release signing PRIVATE keys (docs/design/auto-update.md §15.5). The public half belongs in the
# repository — it is the thing being trusted, so it should be reviewable and attributable to the
# commit that introduced it — and lives as a constant in src/Ui/Updates/ReleaseKeys.cs. The private
# half must never arrive here even for a moment: a key that has been in a git history is a key that
# has to be rotated, and rotating it means every installed copy that carries the old public key
# refuses every release after it. Keep it outside the repository entirely; this is the backstop for
# the day someone runs `ReleaseSigner keygen` in the working directory without thinking.
*.pem
*.key
release-key*
# NOTE: the line above is why packaging/signing-status.sh is not called release-key.sh. A helper
# named to match it is silently untracked, and the two build scripts that source it then fail on a
# fresh clone with nothing to explain why. Keep the pattern broad; name files around it.

# Results produced by running the shipped example workspaces in place. Each example workspace
# carries circuitRF's own .gitignore, which covers *.npy and friends; a Touchstone file written by
# an EM run is not covered by that one and is not a thing to commit either — an example ships its
# design and its EM SETUP, and the user generates the rest.
examples/*/results/
# The TRL example's calibration writes its two fixture files here; they are a result of running it, not an input.
examples/TRL and De-embedding/fixtures/

# Brief em3d-27 F2 spike: wgpu-native binaries (fetched by fetch-wgpu.sh/.ps1) and the regenerated
# F0 case A mesh (data/case.msh, ~15 MB, rebuilt by Gmsh from testdata/em3d/f0/A-bondwire/palace-round/case.geo)
tools/Viewer3dSpike/native/
tools/Viewer3dSpike/data/

# Brief em3d-119 spike harness: its meshes and Palace output (runs/), and a machine's own PATH (env.sh)
tools/palace-symmetry-spike/runs/
tools/palace-symmetry-spike/env.sh
tools/palace-symmetry-spike/oems/ws-*/
tools/palace-symmetry-spike/oems/*.log
tools/palace-symmetry-spike/__pycache__/
tools/palace-symmetry-spike/oems/__pycache__/
# brief-emw-1 spike harness: its EMerge runs (runs/)
tools/emerge-wave-spike/runs/
tools/emerge-wave-spike/__pycache__/

# OpenCASCADE never enters this repository (brief-em3d-61/62, owner decision 2026-09-27): not its source
# archive, not its source tree, not a build tree, not a library. A spike build is ~5 GB. Building
# circuitRF from source fetches OCCT with the recipe (tools/geometry-worker/occt/) into the per-user cache
# outside the working tree. This is the backstop for the day someone builds it in here.
OCCT-*.tar.gz
occt-*.tar.gz
OCCT-[0-9]*/
tools/geometry-worker/build/
# The gdstk worker (brief-oasis-gdstk.md): ensure-built.sh compiles and stages here; gdstk, qhull and zlib
# themselves live only in the per-user cache (tools/gdstk-worker/recipe.env), never in the repository.
tools/gdstk-worker/build/
tools/geometry-worker/occt/build/
tools/geometry-worker/occt/install/
libTK*.dylib
libTK*.so
libTK*.so.*
TK*.dll
TK*.lib
TK*.pdb
/.vscode

# A kit fixture (a SPICE-dialect model folder holding corner files), linked locally — never committed.
/testdata/kit-statistics

# Written by the passive-neighbour EM test run, not an input
/testdata/portcal/results/

# EM-TFR campaign: what a run writes. The references kept are testdata/em-tfr/zigzag/refs/.
/testdata/em-tfr/**/results/
palace.log
